<%!--
The kitchen, served unlisted at /food. Title and body both come from
content/fitness/meals.md, so this template is only the shell: the fitness tab
row, then a contents rail beside the document. Only the top-level parts go in
the rail — Web.Docs anchors the h3 recipes too, so each one can be linked to
directly, but a reader wants a map of the document rather than a list of every
salsa in it.

The tab row still renders, because this page needs a way *out* into the rest of
the fitness section. Nothing in that row points back here: the Kitchen tab was
removed from WebWeb.FitnessSubnav when the page went unlisted, so the links only
run outward. `active` names a tab that no longer exists, which is deliberate and
harmless — the component omits the active tab, and there is none to omit.
--%>
<div class="container meals">
  <WebWeb.FitnessSubnav.subnav active={:food} is_admin={@admin_mode} />

  <article class="glass-panel meals-panel">
    <%!-- The week at a glance — same idea as /england2026's calendar tab, sized
    down to a single row since there's no month to page through, just seven
    repeating days. Plain anchors into the h3s "The week, Sunday to Sunday"
    already carries, so no JS is needed the way England's tab-switch/scroll
    dance was. --%>
    <nav class="week-strip" aria-label="This week's meals">
      <a :for={day <- @week_days} href={"#" <> day.id} class="week-cell">
        <span class="week-cell-day">{day.name}</span>
        <span class="week-cell-icon" aria-hidden="true">{day.icon}</span>
        <span class="week-cell-label">{day.label}</span>
        <span class="week-cell-kcal">{day.kcal} kcal</span>
      </a>
    </nav>

    <div class="meals-layout">
      <nav :if={@contents != []} class="meals-contents" aria-labelledby="meals-contents-title">
        <p id="meals-contents-title" class="meals-contents-title">Contents</p>
        <ol class="meals-contents-list">
          <li :for={entry <- Enum.filter(@contents, &(&1.level == 2))}>
            <a href={"#" <> entry.id}>{entry.text}</a>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <%!-- Styling hangs off `.meals .prose`, so no extra class is needed here. --%>
      <div class="prose">
        {raw(@html_content)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>
